<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Khanate of Ardabil</h1><p class="st-meta">1747 CE – 1808 CE (approx.) · capital Ardabil</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the semi-independent khanates of Azerbaijan after Nader Shah's death, ruled by Shahsevan chiefs from the Safavid holy city of Ardabil.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assassination-of-nader-shah">Nader Shah was assassinated</a> in 1747, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> broke apart and local khans took power across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, home of the shrine of Sheikh Safi al-Din, ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>, chiefs of the Shahsevan tribal confederation established a khanate.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khans relied on Shahsevan nomadic cavalry from the Moghan steppe and the Sabalan mountains. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazar-ali-khan-shahsevan">Nazar Ali Khan Shahsevan</a> was the leading figure of the later 18th century. The khanate was drawn into the conflicts of its neighbours, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quba">Quba</a> khanate, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/talysh-khanate">Talysh Khanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karabakh-khanate">Karabakh Khanate</a>, and was briefly occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatali-khan-of-quba">Fath Ali Khan of Quba</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran</a> imposed control over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> in the 1790s, and by 1808 the khanate had been fully absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> state as a governorate.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/khanate-of-ardabil.jpg" alt="Khanate of Ardabil" width="320" height="296"><figcaption>Image: Sword313 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C6%8Frd%C9%99bil_xanl%C4%B1%C4%9F%C4%B1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1747 CE – 1808 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ardabil</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nazar Ali Khan Shahsevan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Azerbaijani Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ardabil Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1778&amp;focus=khanate-of-ardabil" aria-label="Khanate of Ardabil on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5882 804 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Khanate of Ardabil lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6202 1060L6298 1144"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6298 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6202 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6202 1060h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ardabil. <a href="/atlas?year=1778&amp;focus=khanate-of-ardabil">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

